## Jenkinsfile git commands

Jenkins reads the `Jenkinsfile` from GitHub, so the file must be committed and pushed. If it isn't pushed, Jenkins keeps using the old version or doesn't find it.

### First time

```bash
cd /c/Eclipse/In4Suite_Expenses
nano Jenkinsfile                      # paste the Jenkins groovy script
# press Ctrl + O to save, press Enter, then Ctrl + X to exit
ls -l | grep Jenkinsfile              # verify the file was created
git add Jenkinsfile
git commit -m "Add Jenkinsfile"
git push origin main
```

### Modify / edit

```bash
cd /c/Eclipse/In4Suite_Expenses
ls -l | grep Jenkinsfile              # verify the file exists
nano Jenkinsfile                      # edit the script
# press Ctrl + O to save, press Enter, then Ctrl + X to exit
ls -l | grep Jenkinsfile              # verify again
git add Jenkinsfile
git commit -m "Update Jenkinsfile"
git push origin main
```

> **Tip ★:** if you edit in VS Code instead, run `git add Jenkinsfile`, `git commit -m "Update Jenkinsfile"` and `git push origin main` in its terminal. Then open the file on GitHub to confirm your change is there before building.

